  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading is: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\"\n\nReply A is exactly:\n```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}\n\nReply B is exactly:\n{\"c\":3}\n\nFor Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because the code fence holds at least one object and the last object inside that fence is {\"b\":2}. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {\"c\":3}, because there is no code fence and the last top-level object in the text is {\"c\":3}. Thus the rule requires the check to tell the two replies apart.\n\nThe check, however, gives the same answer for both. In Reply A, the fenced pool captures the entire fence content '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}' as one candidate, which fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the fenced pool yields nothing. The top-level pool then scans the whole text and finds {\"a\":1}, {\"b\":2}, and {\"c\":3}; reversed, it returns {\"c\":3}. In Reply B, the top-level pool finds only {\"c\":3} and returns it. So recover_json_object returns {\"c\":3} for both replies, collapsing the difference the rule requires.\n\nWhat would make me wrong: if recover_json_object returns {\"b\":2} for Reply A and {\"c\":3} for Reply B, or otherwise produces different outputs for the two replies, then the check would not be giving the same answer and my conjecture would be false. This pair is not a repeat of the already-run pair where Reply B had three top-level objects; here Reply B has only the single trailing object {\"c\":3}."
}